 "result": "All of this is in `src/tessel/views.py`.\n\n- **`VerbView`** (L138) is the class you subclass with `get()` and `post()`. It extends the base `View` (L16).\n- **`VerbView.__init_subclass__`** (L165) runs when you define the subclass. It builds `cls.methods` from the HTTP verb methods the class defines (`get` → `\"GET\"`, `post` → `\"POST\"`) plus any inherited ones, unless you set `methods` yourself.\n- **`View.to_view_func(name, ...)`** (L86, classmethod) turns the class into a plain view function.\n  - It creates a new instance per request, or one shared instance if `init_every_request = False`.\n  - It calls `run_view` through `active_app.ensure_sync`, and applies any `decorators`.\n  - It attaches `view_class`, `methods` and `provide_automatic_options` to the function. The route registration reads `methods` from there, so only the verbs you defined are routed.\n- **`VerbView.run_view`** (L182) does the per-request dispatch.\n  - It looks up `getattr(self, request.method.lower())`.\n  - `HEAD` falls back to `get` if there is no `head` handler.\n  - It calls the handler with the URL kwargs.\n\nYou register it with `app.attach_route_rule(\"/path\", view_func=MyView.to_view_func(\"name\"))`.",
